From: tailscale-win7 build
Subject: [PATCH] net/dns: skip the NRPT catch-all when the NRPT database is unavailable

NRPT requires Windows 10. On Windows 7/8 the Windows DNS manager has no
NRPT database, so creating a catch-all rule is not possible. Skip the
catch-all and let the existing interface resolver configuration handle DNS.

diff --git a/net/dns/manager_windows.go b/net/dns/manager_windows.go
--- a/net/dns/manager_windows.go
+++ b/net/dns/manager_windows.go
@@ -433,8 +433,10 @@ func (m *windowsManager) SetDNS(cfg OSConfig) error {
 	if len(cfg.MatchDomains) == 0 {
 		var resolvers []netip.Addr
 		var domains []dnsname.FQDN
-		if !m.disableLocalDNSOverrideViaNRPT() {
+		if !m.disableLocalDNSOverrideViaNRPT() && m.nrptDB != nil {
 			// Create a default catch-all rule to make ourselves the actual primary resolver.
+			// NRPT requires Windows 10; on Windows 7/8/8.1 we skip it and
+			// configure the interface's primary resolver below instead.
 			// Without this rule, Windows 8.1 and newer devices issue parallel DNS requests to DNS servers
 			// associated with all network adapters, even when "Override local DNS" is enabled and/or
 			// a Mullvad exit node is being used, resulting in DNS leaks.
